=== test_agg_raw_crosscheck: summary vs raw, same blocks, same window ===
--- 1. every summary path in src/compute.h is cross-checked here ---
    6 summary path(s) declared, 6 cross-checked
    HOLE pgwt_compute_top_events_from_summaries: N12 (#553): MEASURED CPU. The summary events[] table and the per-query top_events[] list both store WALL for event 0 (summary_writer.c's `se->total_ns += tns`), so this twin's CPU* is the unsplit gap wall and has_measured_cpu is 0, while the raw path reports the measurement plus offcpu_ms. The matrix cannot see it (build_stream is all-UNKNOWN, hole N1), so section 6f pins both answers and the identity between them: raw CPU* + raw off-CPU == summary CPU*. This is a REAL user-visible divergence, not a comparability artefact -- should_use_summaries routes an unfiltered window >= 120 s here, and every UI preset is >= 300 s. The owner's fix is to make the summary twin carry the measurement at summary v9 (with #563's field, one bump); 6f flips one constant then. N9: under a QUERY filter the summary path reads the per-query top_events[] list, which carries no histogram and no max -- so the LATENCY columns come back as 'absent' (has_latency_dist = 0, which the server renders as null). Deliberate, #103, and the only part of this hole left: the missing CPU* row (accum_query_add's `if (old_ev != 0)`, measured raw 7002.426 ms vs summary 2949.426 ms, -57.9%) is FIXED in #317. F_QUERY stays off here only because diff_top_events compares the latency columns too; section 6c compares every other column on that filter row by row AND asserts the latency columns absent on the summary side and present on raw
    HOLE pgwt_compute_top_sessions_from_summaries: N5: the per-second record has no per-session breakdown by query, class or event at all (struct pgwt_summary_session is {pid, db_time_ns, cpu_ns, top_wait_id, top_wait_ns, cpu_offcpu_ns, cpu_measured_records}), so no filter but pid is answerable from summaries and pid is already forced raw. #318: ts_summary_visitor used to read f->pid ONLY and silently return UNFILTERED numbers for the other three, and handle_top_sessions guarded only `query_id == 0`. Now the handler forces raw for all three and the visitor REFUSES them (no rows) as the fail-safe -- section 6d, which also checks the refusal is of the FILTER and not of everything
    HOLE pgwt_compute_top_queries_from_summaries: N10/N11: under a CLASS filter the summary path filters total_ns but adds the query's UNFILTERED sq->count (260 raw vs 390 summary here), and under an EVENT-only filter it accumulates EVERY class into class_ms (class_ms[cpu] 0 raw vs 4053.000 summary). NOT reachable: handle_top_queries (src/server.c:3741,3762) forces raw whenever class_name or event_id is set, so the server only ever takes the two shapes compared here -- unfiltered and query-filtered
    HOLE pgwt_compute_heatmap_from_summaries: N6: the per-second records carry no per-query histogram, so hm_summary_visitor cannot honour a query filter. NOT reachable: handle_heatmap (src/server.c:3968) guards `query_id == 0`
